    "detailedExplanation": "\n<h3>1. Section 118: 'Exchange' Defined</h3>\n<p>'When two persons mutually transfer the ownership of one thing for the ownership of another, <strong>neither thing or both things being money only</strong>, the transaction is called an <strong>exchange</strong>.'</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Mutual Transfer of Ownership:</strong> Each party is a transferor as well as a transferee.</li>\n  <li><strong>Subject-Matter:</strong> Can be movable or immovable property, or both.</li>\n  <li><strong>Price with Money Adjustment (Owelty):</strong> If land is exchanged for land, and one party pays a cash sum (owelty) to equalize values, the transaction is still an exchange, not a sale!</li>\n  <li><strong>Formalities:</strong> Made only in the manner provided for the transfer of such property by sale (Section 118, Para 2). If immovable property is valued at Rs. 100 or more, it requires a <strong>registered instrument</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. Section 119: Right of Party Deprived of Thing Received in Exchange</h3>\n<p>If any party to an exchange is, by reason of defect of title, deprived of the thing or part of the thing received by him, he has two alternative remedies against the other party (unless a contrary intention appears):</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Claim for Compensation:</strong> For any loss caused to him thereby, OR</li>\n  <li><strong>Return of Property:</strong> At his option, require the other party to <strong>return the thing transferred</strong>, if still in the hands of the other party or his legal representative or a transferee without consideration.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. Section 120: Rights and Liabilities of Parties</h3>\n<p>Each party has the rights and is subject to the liabilities of a <strong>seller</strong> as to that which he gives, and has the rights and is subject to the liabilities of a <strong>buyer</strong> as to that which he takes.</p>\n",
